 **Pending validation:** repeat pickup/drop/re-pickup cycles on both Amber cards, including cards lying flat and close to walls; re-grab should be immediate and consistent. Confirm the generous pickup trigger never blocks the world or causes an early reader acceptance. Approach the reader normally with the card and verify the enlarged scan zone removes the need to force the hand/card into nearby solid geometry. Then complete 1/2 -> 2/2, verify exactly-once consumption and travel, and recheck wrong-card rejection, fall recovery, two-client Photon independence and Quest/headset behavior. The apparent reader-area barrier is not considered resolved until the headset/Play Mode retest confirms normal approach; if a hard obstruction remains, inspect the exact wall/gate contact in-scene rather than disabling legitimate world collision globally.
+
+## September 14 continued hand-sticking and keycard pickup failure
+
+**Confirmed runtime report:** Greg reports that touching the box can still stick the hand and produce an intermittent invisible barrier; keycards still take repeated attempts to pick up. This is failed interaction validation. It does not erase the earlier narrowly validated Amber-card 0/2 -> 1/2 submission.
+
+**Source finding / correction:** on PR #23 head `48d94db1de1755d2704e472dba11fec98e1d4956`, Gorilla `Player` movement/head-clearance/unstick raycasts and spherecasts omit an explicit trigger policy while `DynamicsManager.asset` enables `m_QueriesHitTriggers`. Trigger-only volumes on included locomotion layers can therefore be interpreted as movement obstacles. The earlier inference that a trigger-only reader cannot contribute to the apparent barrier is superseded; a lack of solid Rigidbody collision does not exclude query-based locomotion blocking. The exact box collider contacted in Greg's session remains unverified.
+
+**Source finding:** `HeldItemCollisionMode` includes item grab-affordance triggers and XR-rig trigger sensors in its ignored collision pairs, and waits for any of those pairs to separate after release. It also moves trigger-only sensor objects onto `HeldItem`, whose collision matrix excludes the hand/fingertip layers. These are concrete paths that can suppress acquisition/hand-off sensing; they are not proof that every reported failed grab has the same cause.
+
+**Implemented on `feature/keycard-reader-light-feedback`:** this correction adds `QueryTriggerInteraction.Ignore` to all seven Gorilla movement queries, keeps trigger-only sensor GameObjects on their authored layers, restricts local-rig pair suppression and its separation wait to solid colliders, and preserves pre-existing pair ignores owned by other systems. Solid prop/world collision, reader trigger handling, personal-card acceptance, drop recovery, travel and Photon rules are unchanged. The global trigger-query setting is intentionally unchanged.
+
+**Pending validation:** run Source Integrity and Unity 2022.3.55f1 import/compile, then repeat box approach/contact/withdrawal with both hands; ten drop/re-grab cycles per Amber card including flat floor and near-wall drops; immediate re-grab without moving far away; second-hand hover/transfer where the interactable permits it; no held/release shove; normal solid floor/wall contact; no early scanning from grab affordances; and exactly-once 0/2 -> 1/2 -> 2/2 submission/travel. Recheck startup/travel/capture hand reset, physical Hub buttons, Level 2 triggers, two-client Photon and headset comfort. If an invisible barrier remains, identify the actual hit collider and layer in Play Mode rather than disabling environment collision or blindly enlarging volumes. No Unity, headset, or two-client validation is claimed for this patch.
